00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.923
Finally, welcome to
The Rubin Report.

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.193
- David, it's a great
honor to be with you.

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:02.295
You know how much I admire
you, thanks for having me.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:03.563
- Well I'm thrilled
to have you here.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.865 align:start size:84% position:16%
What do you consider
yourself first?

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:08.868
Because you've written what,
like 800 bestselling books.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.737
I mean New York
Times number one,

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:12.338 align:start size:84% position:16%
they couldn't have
been happy about that.

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:15.308 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean what really
are you sort of first?

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:21.514 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- You know, my
attitude about this is

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:23.983 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
I use every platform
I possibly can

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:28.888 align:start size:97% position:3%
to advance the cause of
liberty and constitutionalism

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:32.725 align:start size:84% position:16%
and unalienable rights
since I was a kid.

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.762 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I moved into radio first
which I enjoy the most.

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:40.033
I love to write because
it really gives me time

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.534
to think about things.

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:46.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know to really focus,
think things through.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.375
It's not a fast pace
like radio and so forth

00:01:50.477 --> 00:01:51.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
and of course
television I like too.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.680 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because a lot of
people are very visual.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.148
Unfortunately they
have to look at this,

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:59.385 align:start size:84% position:16%
this punum as we
say but nonetheless,

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.254
I try to use all these formats

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:04.057
to do exactly the same
thing in a different way

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:06.259
and I think our country
is in desperate need

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:11.064
of as many Paul Reveres and
Thomas Paines as possible.

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:13.700 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that's my attitude.
- Yeah.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.101 align:start size:94% position:6%
- And I don't really know.
- Well you are

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:17.003
one of the Paul Reveres
and I wanna get into

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:19.272
some of the stuff that
you've been ahead on

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:20.206 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you've been
kind of warning

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:22.175
about what's happening forever.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:24.410
But let's just back up for a sec

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.945
because I realized this morning

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.480
as I was doing a
little research,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.983
I actually don't know that
much about your childhood

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I sense, you
strike me as sort of,

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:34.854
you were conservative at five.

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:37.223
Is that possible, is that right?

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:38.892
- Well it started
in a log cabin.

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:40.393 align:start size:84% position:16%
No.
- Yeah, with the guy

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:42.462
behind you I suspect.
- I think, that yeah.

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:43.563
No that's correct.

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:46.266
Look, here's the thing.

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:48.034 align:start size:88% position:13%
I never liked authority,
that's a funny thing.

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:50.470
I never liked authority,
I didn't like classrooms,

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.539
I didn't like how
strict they were.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.576
I was beyond it, I was ready
to do my own thing as a kid.

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:58.811
I was ready to read my
own books, check history.

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:01.047 align:start size:97% position:3%
We didn't have the internet
and so forth and I,

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:02.549
that's exactly what I did.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:07.387
And I didn't understand why
I had to do stupid things

00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:10.323
just because that was
the regiment that I had

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:14.460
and so I think that had
sort of an intellectual

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.497 align:start size:88% position:13%
or philosophical effect
on me unwittingly

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:21.100
and as I got older I
really did distrust

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.303
what people were telling me.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:24.971 align:start size:84% position:16%
Not that I was
paranoid or anything,

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:27.307 align:start size:84% position:16%
it was like I
wanted to check it.

00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:29.809 align:start size:84% position:16%
I wanted to make
sure I agreed with it

00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:30.877
and sometimes I'd say,

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:31.978
"Well what is that all about?

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:33.179
"I don't agree with that.

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:34.647
"What about this?"

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:39.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
And my general philosophy
is live and let live.

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:41.387
That's pretty much
my general philosophy

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:42.722
and get out of my face.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.357
That's pretty much it.

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:48.228
And it's gotten a little bit
more complicated since then

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:52.165 align:start position:0%
but I think I was a
libertarian at a very young age

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.033 align:start size:88% position:13%
and more of a
libertarian conservative

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.735
and then I kind of
coined this phrase

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:59.639
or made it public again which
is constitutional conservative

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:01.107
20 years ago I said.

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:04.177 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because I was referring to
the Constitution all the time.

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:06.279 align:start size:88% position:13%
But yeah we would,
around the dinner table,

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:08.147
around the breakfast table.

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.183 align:start size:88% position:13%
There were two brothers
and my parents.

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:14.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
We were very talkative,
we would debate the issues

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.657 align:start position:0%
and I'll tell you something
Dave, none of us were liberals.

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:18.725
Even though we're all Jewish

00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:20.260
and so none of us were liberals.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:24.998
My father passed away, my
mother who passed away.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:30.570 align:start size:94% position:6%
My father told me he voted
for Goldwater in 1964.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.639
Now how many Jewish
people do you know

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:34.407
who voted for Goldwater in 1964?

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:36.209
And I said to him,

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:37.644
"Dad, why did you do that?"

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:40.280
And he said and I'll quote him,

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.849
"I knew all the bullshit they
were saying about Goldwater

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:45.718 align:start size:84% position:16%
"couldn't be true
so I voted for him."

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:48.321
And that was kind of my
attitude at a young age

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:51.624
and you know, and then
you add meat to the bone.

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:53.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
You start reading
philosophy and history

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:57.163
which I can't tell you
how many books I've read.

00:04:57.163 --> 00:04:59.932
Economics and that sort of thing

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:04.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you develop a way of
living and a way of thinking.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.105
- Yeah, let's dive a
little deeper on that

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:08.308
'cause you mentioned
sort of libertarian

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:11.778 align:start size:97% position:3%
to conservative to
Constitutional conservative.

00:05:11.778 --> 00:05:14.714
What was it, as you know I've
sort of brought the phrase

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:17.550 align:start position:0%
classical liberal back and
that was what my book was about

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.985
and people always say to me,

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:21.087 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Well Dave, is there any
really meaningful distinction

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:23.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
"between classical
liberal and libertarian?"

00:05:23.423 --> 00:05:24.691
So actually, let
me start with that.

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:26.793 align:start size:84% position:16%
Do you see any
meaningful distinction

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:28.494
or have the labels and words

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:31.764 align:start size:88% position:13%
all become sort of
muddled in modern times?

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:34.567
- Well they can be.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:38.137
But they're in the right area.

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:39.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
Other words, not
right philosophical.

00:05:39.806 --> 00:05:41.407 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- In the correct area.

00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:46.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know I've always,
libertarian conservative,

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:49.482
libertarian conservative,
conservative libertarian.

00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:52.085
It's like Scalia and Thomas.

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:56.022
They both wrote opinions,
sometimes they would agree,

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:57.890
sometimes they would disagree.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:01.127
But the way they approached
the issue was what is key.

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:02.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
What is it that
drives your thinking?

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:05.198 align:start size:88% position:13%
What are your principles
behind your thinking?

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:08.568
And so, I was once asked.

00:06:08.568 --> 00:06:11.037 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think it was by Ben
as a matter on his show.

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:14.841 align:start size:97% position:3%
He said, "When you
interpret the Constitution,

00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:16.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
"do you do it from a
libertarian point of view,

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:19.679 align:start position:0%
"or a conservative point of
view or how do you approach it?"

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:21.114
I said,

00:06:21.114 --> 00:06:24.083 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Well libertarianism and
conservatism are philosophies.

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:26.419
"Interpreting the Constitution

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.788
"is interpreting
the Constitution."

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:32.158
That is, you're not supposed
to bring a philosophy to it.

00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:36.129 align:start size:97% position:3%
You're supposed to try and
discern what the framers meant

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:38.598
and what the words
meant at the time.

00:06:38.598 --> 00:06:40.032
So I don't get into this,

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:42.702
am I a conservative,
liberal, libertarian

00:06:42.702 --> 00:06:46.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I interpret the
Constitution, it's
the Constitution.

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:49.175 align:start size:97% position:3%
So why do I call myself a
Constitutional conservative?

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:51.144
I have many libertarian beliefs.

00:06:52.879 --> 00:06:55.815 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I call myself a
Constitutional conservative

00:06:55.815 --> 00:06:59.218
because of the thinking and
the arguments and so forth

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:00.486
that people understand

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:02.855
that went behind the
Constitution itself.

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:05.591
Debates oh well we
don't want a mob,

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.860
we don't want a monarchy,
we don't want these things.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.063 align:start position:0%
We don't want direct democracy,
we don't want factions.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:13.065
What do we want,
we want a republic.

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:15.034
And they developed
the most fascinating

00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:20.139
and I hope lasting republic
that man has ever created.

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.241
So are there differences?

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:24.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well of course
there're differences.

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:26.946 align:start size:97% position:3%
Libertarians fight with
conservatives all the time.

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:29.816
Guys like Hayek and Nietzsche,

00:07:29.816 --> 00:07:31.717
they had no stomach
for conservatives.

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:34.720
The Austrian school.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.689
On the other hand,
Milton Friedman did.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:41.828
He's one of my great heroes
and so we're gonna argue

00:07:41.828 --> 00:07:45.264
over libertarian versus
conservative, I'm fine.

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:48.634 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're gonna argue over
Americanism versus Marxism,

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:50.236
that's a whole nother.

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:51.537
- I think we're gonna be okay

00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:53.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
on whatever the
marginal libertarian,

00:07:53.706 --> 00:07:54.841 align:start size:97% position:3%
conservative differences are.
- Right.

00:07:54.841 --> 00:07:55.875
- So let's put that aside.

00:07:55.875 --> 00:07:57.143
But you know,

00:07:57.143 --> 00:07:59.078
every way that you just
answered that question.

00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:00.813
What sort of sat beneath it was

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:03.349
you have great reverence
for the founding documents

00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:06.385
for the founders, for our
history and it seems to me

00:08:06.385 --> 00:08:09.489
we live in a time
where that is just,

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:10.556
you actually said hope.

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:12.058
That was meaning that you hope

00:08:12.058 --> 00:08:13.526
that these things
will still matter.

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:15.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
But we in essence
live in a time where

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:19.665
we're erasing all of that at
a rapidly increasing pace.

00:08:22.068 --> 00:08:24.070 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah and I'll get
into this a little bit,

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:24.904 align:start size:81% position:19%
I've been thinking
about this too.

00:08:24.904 --> 00:08:26.706
We are and why is that?

00:08:26.706 --> 00:08:29.809
Why is it that the world
we're told in America now

00:08:29.809 --> 00:08:34.113
has begun 1619 and not 1776.

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:38.818 align:start size:97% position:3%
Critical race theory,
critical immigration theory.

00:08:40.553 --> 00:08:44.190
The attack on capitalism
with the degrowth movement,

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:45.258
the climate change movement.

00:08:45.258 --> 00:08:46.859
All these things, what are they,

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:48.594
why do they kind
of just spring up

00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:52.732
in the last 20, 30 years and
especially our focus this year

00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:53.966
and last year?

00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:57.236
And the reason is, and
I'm gonna use the word.

00:08:57.236 --> 00:08:59.705 align:start size:88% position:13%
These are Marxist-based
ideologies.

00:08:59.705 --> 00:09:02.141
If people understand Karl Marx,

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:03.409
they understand his writings,

00:09:03.409 --> 00:09:04.877
they understand his background.

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:06.445
Now I've studied him
very very thoroughly

00:09:06.445 --> 00:09:09.448
for a project I'm working
on, he was a journalist.

00:09:09.448 --> 00:09:10.917
People don't even know
he was a journalist.

00:09:10.917 --> 00:09:13.719 align:start size:97% position:3%
He had many articles that
appeared in the New York Sun.

00:09:15.087 --> 00:09:18.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was a classical liberal
for a period of time

00:09:18.357 --> 00:09:20.993 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then something
happened, he saw things

00:09:20.993 --> 00:09:25.831 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he abandoned it and then
he developed his ideology

00:09:25.831 --> 00:09:29.302
really based on Rousseau
and Hegel and those guys

00:09:29.302 --> 00:09:32.972
and he just moved it
towards materialism

00:09:32.972 --> 00:09:33.806 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he just, he was an angry
man, he was a poor man.

00:09:36.943 --> 00:09:39.078
He always relied on
his lawyer father

00:09:39.078 --> 00:09:41.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
and his friend Engels
to subsidize him.

00:09:43.249 --> 00:09:45.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think, and by the
way, people don't realize.

00:09:45.551 --> 00:09:48.588 align:start size:97% position:3%
For 30, 40 years after he
died nobody cared about Marx

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:53.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
except American progressives
who were fixated with him

00:09:54.827 --> 00:09:59.832
but all that said, I'm
of the attitude that

00:10:01.067 --> 00:10:05.137 align:start size:97% position:3%
with the early
progressives, Dewey, Wilson,

00:10:05.137 --> 00:10:10.142
Crowley, all these guys and
more, they said basically,

00:10:11.277 --> 00:10:13.512
"We need to change
education in America.

00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:15.748
"It's not enough to teach
mathematics and history

00:10:15.748 --> 00:10:19.185
"and English without
a social construct,

00:10:19.185 --> 00:10:20.386
"without an understanding

00:10:20.386 --> 00:10:22.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
"and putting in a
cultural perspective."

00:10:23.255 --> 00:10:24.457
And that's what they did.

00:10:24.457 --> 00:10:27.326 align:start size:88% position:13%
They fundamentally
altered public education

00:10:27.326 --> 00:10:28.494
which became bigger and bigger

00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:30.496 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then they
altered colleges and,

